# c2.rules — Command-and-Control signatures (the fast-path layer for "Exfil / C2").
#
# Signature/protocol-anomaly companion to the behavioral Zeek scripts in
# ../zeek/{dns-c2,reverse-tunnel,icmp-tunnel,tls-c2}.zeek. Suricata is the wrong tool
# for volumetrics (bytes/duration/inter-arrival) — those stay in Zeek — so this file
# carries the per-packet/per-flow tells Suricata does well: encoded DNS labels,
# oversized ICMP payloads, and known-implant TLS fingerprints. Together they close the
# Command-and-Control (TA0011) tactic that was scaffolded here but empty.
#
# Mirrors the htpx C2 pairs in dotfiles-Kali PURPLE-TEAM.md (dns-tunnel-c2,
# icmp-tunnel-c2, mtls-c2-sliver). Validate by running the matching implant/tunnel
# from dotfiles-Kali (hacktheplanet "Exfil / C2" folds).
#
# sids are in the same local/custom range as coercion.rules (9_000_1xx here);
# renumber if it collides with your ruleset. These are alert-and-investigate, not
# drop — each shape has benign edge cases (see the per-rule notes).

# ── DNS tunneling (T1071.004) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Encoded payload rides the leftmost label: one long high-entropy label is nothing
# like a hostname a human types. Behavioral volume/entropy lives in dns-c2.zeek;
# this is the single-query tell for the egregiously long label. Tune the length and
# baseline out any app that legitimately encodes long subdomains (some CDNs, ACME
# DNS-01 challenges, cloud health-checks).
alert dns $HOME_NET any -> any any (msg:"DOTFILES-DEFENSE DNS tunneling - long encoded label"; dns.query; pcre:"/^[a-z0-9+\/=_-]{45,}\./i"; classtype:bad-unknown; metadata:mitre_tactic_id TA0011, mitre_technique_id T1071.004; sid:9000101; rev:1;)

# Very long total query name (many encoded labels) is the other tunnel shape.
alert dns $HOME_NET any -> any any (msg:"DOTFILES-DEFENSE DNS tunneling - oversized query name"; dns.query; pcre:"/^.{120,}$/"; classtype:bad-unknown; metadata:mitre_tactic_id TA0011, mitre_technique_id T1071.004; sid:9000102; rev:1;)

# ── ICMP tunneling (T1095) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# OS echo payloads are small and fixed (32-64B on Windows/Linux). A large echo
# payload carries a tunneled session (icmpsh/ptunnel/Loki). Volume lives in
# icmp-tunnel.zeek; this flags the oversized packet on its own. itype 8 = echo
# request, 0 = echo reply.
alert icmp $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"DOTFILES-DEFENSE ICMP tunneling - oversized echo request"; itype:8; dsize:>800; classtype:bad-unknown; metadata:mitre_tactic_id TA0011, mitre_technique_id T1095; sid:9000103; rev:1;)
alert icmp $EXTERNAL_NET any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"DOTFILES-DEFENSE ICMP tunneling - oversized echo reply"; itype:0; dsize:>800; classtype:bad-unknown; metadata:mitre_tactic_id TA0011, mitre_technique_id T1095; sid:9000104; rev:1;)

# ── Encrypted C2 by TLS fingerprint (T1573.002) ──────────────────────────────
# Known-implant JA3 hashes are a high-fidelity fast path, but they ROTATE, so they are
# NOT hard-coded here: the rule below reads them from a `dataset` file that
# ../update-ja3-feed.sh regenerates from a maintained feed (abuse.ch SSLBL JA3 by
# default). ja3-c2.lst holds base64 of each ja3.hash string, one per line; empty = empty
# set = matches nothing, so it can't misfire on stale hashes. tls-c2.zeek carries the
# self-signed-cert behavioral fallback for the unknown-implant case no hash list covers.
#
# Ships COMMENTED because `ja3.hash` requires `app-layer.protocols.tls.ja3-fingerprints:
# yes` in suricata.yaml — an un-enabled ja3 keyword fails rule load. To enable: turn ja3
# on, run ../update-ja3-feed.sh, fix the `load` path to your ja3-c2.lst, and uncomment.
# alert tls $HOME_NET any -> $EXTERNAL_NET any (msg:"DOTFILES-DEFENSE C2 known JA3 (maintained feed)"; flow:to_server,established; ja3.hash; dataset:isset,ja3-c2,type string,load ja3-c2.lst; classtype:trojan-activity; metadata:mitre_tactic_id TA0011, mitre_technique_id T1573.002; sid:9000105; rev:1;)
